# 220: who he is.
{220}{}{Jericho. I used to take what I wanted off people like you. Then the knees went. Now I live in a town that needs a man who can hit something at two hundred yards. They don't like me. They like the two hundred yards.}

# 230 to 237: the tip.
{230}{}{Might be I know where Moriarty keeps something that isn't his. Twenty dollars.}
{231}{}{Twenty. Talk.}
{232}{}{Not for twenty.}
{233}{}{Back room. Strongbox by his bed. It's where he keeps what he swears he's paying Gob and the girl: a hundred and eighty, by the count I heard through the wall. Door's locked. The key lives on his belt.}
{234}{}{Strongbox. Back room. By the bed. A hundred and eighty. Key's on his belt. You want it sung?}
{235}{}{You haven't got twenty. Then it stays worth knowing.}
{236}{}{I'm a little short.}
{237}{}{Good to know.}

# 240: the town. 241: the same once the sheriff is dead.
{240}{}{Sheep, with a wall round them. A sheriff who thinks a hat is a law, and a church that prays to the thing that'll kill it. Best town I ever lived in. Nobody shoots at me before breakfast.}
{241}{}{It had a sheriff. Now it has a hat with nobody under it, and a boy who thinks he'll grow into it. Give it a month. I've seen what moves in when the law moves out. I used to be it.}

# 250 to 255: his past.
{250}{}{Somebody ought to learn to whisper. ...Yeah. I rode. I burned things. I buried friends in ditches. You want me to cry about it, or you want to buy me a drink?}
{251}{}{I'll buy the drink.}
{252}{}{Neither.}
{253}{}{[He takes the money without looking at it.] Huh. You're not the worst thing that walked in here today.}
{254}{}{I could use a man like you.}
{255}{}{I'm retired. Come back when you've burned something bigger than a bridge. Then I'll think about telling you no again.}

# 260, 261: low Intelligence.
